five years ago  lamed filmmaker Alejandro Gonzalez  Inarritu embarked on making a bold new  work to bring empathy and compassion for  the immigrants who cross our southern  border as they flee the poverty and  violence of their homelands knowing that  one of the most powerful things drama  can do is to have us walk in the  footsteps of others Alejandra wanted to  take this to a whole new level having  this literally walk in the footsteps of  these immigrants using the emerging  technology of virtual reality as the  centerpiece of a 3-tier multimedia  installation with the help of his  longtime colleague cinematographer  Emmanuel Lubezki of producer Mary parent  and visual effects house ILM and using  the Testaments of hundreds of immigrants  Alejandro has created an interactive  experience called Carinae lladhina flesh  and sand that puts us in the desert at  dawn walking barefoot with immigrants  risking their lives as they are hunted  by the u.s. Border Patrol the rectangle  of the screen has vanished and we are  surrounded by sirens lights assault  rifles the harsh orders of border patrol  officers the anguished screams and  weeping of the men women and children  around us we sit on metal benches as  captured immigrants do in the freezing  holding cells of detention centers later  we walk down a corridor lined with  actual pieces of the rusted border fence  where we see the portraits of the people  we have just been traveling with and  learned their heartbreaking stories my  fellow governor director Michael Mann  said that by putting us in the skin of  this experience seeing with the  immigrants eyes and feeling their  distress  with an intensity never done before  carne yah denna is to virtual reality  what Eisenstein's Battleship Potemkin  was to film carne denna is a true drama  urging us to not build walls of hatred  and fear but to build bridges of  compassion and understanding  [Applause]
 

